# File Storage

This app ships with a storage manager backed by `github.com/goforj/storage`.

The generated setup uses:

- one default disk
- many named disks
- the same API regardless of backend

## Defaults

By default, the scaffold defines:

- the default disk from `STORAGE_*` -> `storage/app/private`
- a named `public` disk from `STORAGE_PUBLIC_*` -> `storage/app/public`

The generated app can also define additional named disks through `STORAGE_<NAME>_*` variables.

## Configuration

Use these environment variables:

```env
STORAGE_DRIVER=local
STORAGE_ROOT=storage/app/private
STORAGE_PREFIX=

STORAGE_PUBLIC_DRIVER=local
STORAGE_PUBLIC_ROOT=storage/app/public

STORAGE_AVATARS_DRIVER=s3
STORAGE_AVATARS_BUCKET=my-app-uploads
STORAGE_AVATARS_REGION=us-east-1
STORAGE_AVATARS_PREFIX=avatars
```

Supported drivers in the scaffold resolver:

- `local`
- `memory`
- `redis`
- `ftp`
- `sftp`
- `s3`
- `gcs`
- `dropbox`
- `rclone`

## Driver Optimization

Storage drivers are added to the generated app on demand.

When you run:

```bash
forj generate --storage
```

GoForj inspects:

- `STORAGE_*` for the default disk
- `STORAGE_<NAME>_*` for named disks

Then it generates storage config code that imports only the drivers your app is actually using.

Examples:

- local-only apps pull only `localstorage`
- local + s3 apps pull `localstorage` and `s3storage`
- apps that add `dropbox`, `gcs`, or `rclone` pull those driver modules only when needed

This keeps the rendered app lean while still supporting the full driver set.

`forj build` also runs storage generation automatically before building the app binary.

## Usage

```go
disk := app.Storage().Default()
err := disk.Put("avatars/1.txt", []byte("hello"))
```

Resolve a named disk:

```go
avatars := app.Storage().Avatars()
public := app.Storage().Public()
```

## Notes

- Local disk paths are resolved relative to the app root unless you provide an absolute path.
- Disk `prefix` values scope all paths on a disk beneath that prefix.
- Run `forj generate --storage` after adding or renaming named disks when you want to refresh storage code directly.
- `forj generate` also regenerates storage accessors and storage driver imports.
- `forj build` runs the same storage generation step automatically before building.
